\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this value in the second and first equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{2}-2(5)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{1}+5=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=5,$ which means \n\n$$\n3{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+4+5=12.\n$$" "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this value in the second and first equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{2}-2(5)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{1}+5=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=5,$ which means \n\n$$\n3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+5{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+4+5=12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345006 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$  by inspection."] [:db/cas 17592186345006 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=2\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+0\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345007 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection." "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ by inspection."] [:db/cas 17592186345007 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=2\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+0\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345008 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ ${a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345008 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$" "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 